WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:09.220
And so good morning to everyone coming over
or dialing in over from the East Coast of

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.620
the United States.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.320
And good evening to the brave souls that are
dialing in from Europe.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.320
So jack already introduced me, so I'm going
to skip this.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.540
But today we're going to talk about standards.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.369
And before
I go deeper into the particular use cases

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:35.530
in biomedical imaging, I'd like to talk a
bit more broadly about what

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.250
standards are.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:48.680
So to begin with--
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:56.910
Well, if you haven't understood any of this,
this is probably because we have not been

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:02.300
using the same standard,
because if you look at a language as a form

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.439
of a standard, or in other words a standard
is a form of a language,

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:07.990
it's all about communicating.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:14.700
It's all about finding the same level playing
field to be able to exchange ideas.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.990
And
when we are able to do that, we are able to

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.130
build things together.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.829
It's basically the essence of collaboration.

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:28.630
And languages, even though they usually evolve
over hundreds of years, are a form of a standard.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.729
I subscribe to
you communicating with you using English.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:36.990
And only because of that, you are able to
do this, because you have

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.749
also understood the rules of that language.

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:44.549
And even though languages evolve over time--
over, as I mentioned,

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:51.389
ages or millennia some of them actually have
been designed de novo, from scratch in other

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:52.609
words.

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:55.560
For example,
Esperanto, which is not an official language,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.119
created 19-- sorry, 1870-- which was optimized
for how easy it should

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:09.649
be to pick it up and learn, which is just
a neat example of how people introduce artificial

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:12.430
standards in languages as
well.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:17.819
But languages are not the only examples of
standard out there.

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:19.959
Probably each of you have a computer.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.240
And that
computer has a platter of different parts

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.709
and connections you can plug into.

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:29.819
And this is another very common
occurrence of standards.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:37.569
And those connections are industry, acknowledged,
approved way of communicating

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:39.379
between hardware.

00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:44.189
And probably you recognize some of the parts
in this picture.

00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:49.680
And this is how I can buy a piece
of hardware, for example a laptop, from one

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.000
manufacturer, and then buy a microphone or
a webcam from another

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.969
manufacturer, and plug one into another.

00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:01.069
And those two manufacturers, they do not have
to work with each other

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.530
directly.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:09.180
They just need to subscribe to the same language,
to the same way of exchanging information

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.870
by a
hardware connection.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:17.099
And that's how all these different plaques
and parts come along.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:24.709
And there are more examples, especially with
the hardware industry, where standardization

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:28.650
doesn't always go
that well.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:35.620
For example, many of the laptops that are
coming out right now have this new, fancy

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.159
part, sometimes
referred to as USB C, sometimes referred to--

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.450
Thunderbolt 3.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.450
And this part looks exactly the same, even
though it

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:49.790
could actually provide many different functionalities
that are not totally compatible.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:57.499
So that also shows that even in
the industry hardware world, there is a need

00:03:57.499 --> 00:04:03.390
for standards that are clean, as well as intuitive,
both for users and

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.709
the manufacturers.

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:06.779
We can go further.

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:12.930
For example, the way we deliver electricity
is another form of standardization that is

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.879
very
important.

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:20.959
So for any of you who traveled a little bit
outside of your country, you probably noticed

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.430
that the sockets
in the walls that deliver power have different

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.440
shapes.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.250
And most of North America, it's a specific
standard from

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.450
NEMA.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:40.139
And most of Europe is also subscribing to
a different standard.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:44.880
And of course there's the UK that has to be
different.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.590
But that's another way of benefiting from
doing one thing the same way, because if we

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:56.300
didn't have a
common way of delivering power to our appliances,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.280
each manufacturer of appliance would have
to basically find

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:07.199
some sort of a deal with a power provider
or become the power provider itself, which

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:09.150
is preposterous.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:16.110
And then we have digital communication, so
in other words the internet.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:24.000
And that's another example where
agreeing on a common standard of communicating

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.860
and encapsulating information in packets allows
many

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:36.450
services built by different companies, that
sometimes outright compete with each other,

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:40.850
to operate on the same
layer and be able to communicate with each

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:41.850
other.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:46.919
For example, both Bing and Google are using
the same

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:53.860
TCP/IP protocol to communicate with their
customers and with each other, even though

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:57.430
they're are at outright
competition.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:04.400
And then we also have a higher level of digital
communication, the application programming

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.710
interfaces, or APIs.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:14.240
This is the way how modern web applications
can be built very rapidly, and you can go

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:19.520
from prototype to a
scalable solution very quickly by using those

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:27.919
standardized ways of scheduling services and
receiving results from

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:29.389
a plethora of different things.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:33.870
Right now, we have APIs for a very sophisticated
procedure, for example, detecting

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:42.259
faces from pictures or detecting mood of a
particular person from a recording of their

00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:43.259
voice.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:49.070
So this thing has been
flourishing a lot.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:57.180
So you might ask how these standards actually
developed in the industry, how all these advantages

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.560
of having a
common language actually come about.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:11.610
So in the industry, there are a few organizations
that try to help the

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:17.099
different industry players to start talking
with the same language.

00:07:17.099 --> 00:07:19.289
One of them is the Institute of Electrical
and

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:25.659
Electronics Engineers, IEEE in other words,
which is known in some circles for publishing

00:07:25.659 --> 00:07:26.830
engineering content.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:31.630
But
one of the big things they do is working on

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.639
standards.

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:43.419
So one of the standards that they're very
famous for is Wi-Fi, or in other words 802.11,

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:50.159
which is an extension of
the 802 standard, which is just ethernet.

00:07:50.159 --> 00:07:53.939
And that standard allows different appliances
to talk to each other

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:56.469
wirelessly.

00:07:56.469 --> 00:08:02.190
And this standard was developed by EEE by
bringing different industry players into the

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.849
same process.

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:12.689
And IEEE has a process for deciding what should
be in the final standards.

00:08:12.689 --> 00:08:21.419
And this process involve a draft that is
then refined, that is then voted upon.

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:26.750
And if it has enough votes, it's basically
becomes a standard.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:33.380
And IEEE is acknowledging the role of different
players in the industry.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:39.720
So here when it actually comes to voting in
many occasions, it's not people voting for

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.810
a standard, but particular key industry players
voting for what should be-

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.380
- in the standard and what should not.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:52.390
Another example of a standardization body
is the World Wide Web Consortium.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:09:00.650
So this one is very well known for
higher-level markup language that we use on

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.830
the internet almost everyday.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:12.630
So coming from the very commonly
used HTML, which then became XHTML, through

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:17.500
styling-- such as CSS and Images like scale-vector
graphics--

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:24.090
all of these things where standards proposed
and working by the World Wide Web Consortium.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:32.020
And right now they
enable web developers to build websites and

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:37.230
be relatively certain that those websites
will work and be rendered in

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:40.760
a similar way across different web browsers.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.080
And those who did web development know that
actually adherence to those standards by different

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.870
web browsers
has been historically problematic.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:57.130
And quite often there are some rough edges
that require developers to work

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:04.160
around certain problems, particular tools
that do not implement those standards very

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.230
well.

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:15.450
And World Wide Web Consortium also has a process,
and it also involves multiple drafts and release

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:20.060
candidates
that are either advanced to the next stage

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.210
or refined.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.580
And these multiple levels of proposal is important
because

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:33.760
each time you elevate a particular proposal
to a new level, that rings a lot of bells,

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.640
and that promotes a lot of
players to contribute time and look at the

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.840
standard and basically-- it's basically a
signal saying hey, this will

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.610
become a standard soon.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:52.800
You should have a look at it now before it's
too late to make any changes.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.690
And then finally-- something much more relevant
to biomedical engineering-- there's the National

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:02.770
Electrical
Manufacturers Association, or NEMA, which

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:09.730
is not only famous for standardizing the electrical
plugs in the US, but

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:16.210
also for the DICOM standards, so digital imaging
and communications in medicine standard, which

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:21.800
is widely used
in clinical applications for storing and transferring

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:28.080
medical imaging data-- has been used for quite
a while.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:33.620
I think
history reaches the 80s, and it has seen a

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:34.620
lot of revisions.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.110
But it is widely adopted in different hospital
settings,

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:45.050
even though adherence to this standard is
sometimes not perfect, especially when it

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:50.330
comes to neuroimaging, and
even more when it comes to cutting edge research

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.860
neuroimaging.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.670
But that's the industry.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:12:00.000
And I want to talk a little bit more about
standards and context of research or in context

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.020
of
academia.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:09.180
So your situation is slightly different, and
I would like to explain where are those differences

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.830
coming
from.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:18.910
But before we actually go into the cultural
changes, I would like to point you to this

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:25.410
fantastic resource, this
website called fairsharing dot-org, which

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:29.660
allows you to search across different research
or academic standards

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:31.850
that are out there.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:41.620
So if you are-- if you would like to for certain
products, procedures, or databases-- you would

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:47.040
like to adhere to a standard and basically
see what other-- how other people try to solve

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.750
the problem you're
struggling with, this might be a great point

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:57.670
to start, basically, looking for standards
that are already developed in a

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.590
particular field that you're interested in.

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:06.880
OK, so let's talk a little bit how different
standards at academia are.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:14.240
Quite often, standards in academia come from
a sort of bottom-up way.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:23.490
Rarely, we have these organizations that are
basically imposing a standard on everyone

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:24.490
else.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:32.510
It's quite often a few developers-- that's
actually how NIFTI was coming around-- that

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:38.600
see the need for a
better exchange of information and less error-prone

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:43.810
ways of transferring metadata.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:50.470
And quite often the standards are developed
with a specific project in mind.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.720
So for example-- which is very good.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:59.770
They are there to solve a particular problem
for a particular project, which is good in

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:04.760
a way that it is driven by a
practical use case.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:12.560
But it's a little bit more limited, because
often those problems are constrained or limited

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:18.760
by
time, by grants, by a particular specific

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:23.200
flavor of this problem in this particular,
specific project.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:31.800
An open fMRI, for example-- a repository of
imaging data I've been involved with-- had

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:32.800
the same thing.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.450
They
needed to have some sort of formalized way

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:42.960
of storing data, and they just came up with
something for that

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:51.460
particular project without involving broader
community or negotiations or any consensus

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:52.460
building.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.290
And that's
perfectly fine.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:15:03.020
And this comes from the broader incentive
structure in academia, where you basically

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:07.570
are rewarded
for solving particular, specific problems,

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:11.290
rather than building consensus or brother
collaboration.

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:19.670
Standards academia also tend to be technically
simple, because-- especially in biomedical

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:26.240
imaging, quite often
you have a mix of different levels of technical

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:31.000
expertise, and that has to be accounted for.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:36.430
And therefore standards
need to be, basically, simpler, so people

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:41.490
whose expertise lies more in the biology could
also benefit from using

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:49.750
them and not require getting a CS degree to
start building and working on top of a particular

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:51.130
standard.

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:54.770
And sometimes standards tend to be competitive
in academia.

00:15:54.770 --> 00:16:00.080
So it is a bit unfortunate that the way scientists
are

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:07.380
rewarded it is for making particular neural
findings.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:10.830
And quite often that leads to competitiveness.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:14.980
And that leads
to a particular group trying to do something

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.120
before another group.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:22.910
And the reward only goes to the first person
that does something.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:30.450
And that's why sometimes we do see some fights
between standards, and we are seeing

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:37.390
those fights being fueled by basically the
incentive structure and how people are rewarded

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:40.730
through grants.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:47.580
And then we also see the same benefits as
with standardization effort.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:52.790
When a particular standard gets widely
adopted, there are great benefits, because

00:16:52.790 --> 00:17:02.220
suddenly we can combine different tools and
different-- sorry, different

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:06.289
software tools because they start talking
with the same language.

00:17:06.289 --> 00:17:09.100
So an example of that is Nifty and neuroimaging
research.

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:13.639
This is a imaging file format, which is very
widely

00:17:13.639 --> 00:17:22.159
adopted right now, even-- despite some of
its shortcomings.

00:17:22.159 --> 00:17:29.320
But because it's implemented in so many different
neuroimaging-- research neuroimaging pieces

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:36.879
of software, we are able to mix and match
them and build optimal

00:17:36.879 --> 00:17:40.190
pipelines by picking and choosing algorithms
from different sources.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:45.269
So this is great, and this is something that
basically applies both in academia and in

00:17:45.269 --> 00:17:47.590
the industry.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:51.350
When we all start talking the same language,
there are

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:53.950
great benefits to come.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:18:01.039
OK, so after this brief introduction-- I would
like to talk about the practical use case,

00:18:01.039 --> 00:18:04.230
and this is something that I'm
a bit more knowledgeable about because it's

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.500
a movement I've been spearheading.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:13.399
It's basically an attempt to
standardize how we describe and annotate research

00:18:13.399 --> 00:18:17.289
human neuroimaging data.

00:18:17.289 --> 00:18:23.149
And I will try to make it-- make
some broader points because I fully understand

00:18:23.149 --> 00:18:27.389
that many of you are coming from different
subfields of clinical

00:18:27.389 --> 00:18:33.169
research and not necessarily interested in
the brain and imaging.

00:18:33.169 --> 00:18:38.840
But I feel that there are some take-home
messages from this attempt that could be applied

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:40.580
to different subfields.

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:46.740
And maybe some of you will be engaged
in standardization effort and different academic

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.429
subfields in the future.

00:18:48.429 --> 00:18:51.740
And maybe some of this advice would be
useful for you.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:54.490
So let me give you some introduction.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:19:01.279
First of all, and I think it's important for
any project you start-- is to think

00:19:01.279 --> 00:19:03.100
about your goals.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:07.889
So what are you actually trying to solve,
because it's so easy to go into the nitty-gritty

00:19:07.889 --> 00:19:12.399
details
and start solving technical puzzles and forget

00:19:12.399 --> 00:19:18.159
about the particular big-picture problem that
you are actually tasked

00:19:18.159 --> 00:19:19.370
to solve.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:28.740
So we wanted to enable reuse of research neuroimaging
data, and we wanted to make this applicable

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:33.009
both to data shared within a lab and between
labs.

00:19:33.009 --> 00:19:39.779
So the stress here is on the reuse of data,
so this is not just describing data for the

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:43.850
sake of having a complete
description of it.

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:51.530
And this is having in mind the actual application,
the fact that someone generates a data set,

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:54.000
and
someone else takes this data set and tries

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.870
to extract knowledge from it.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:59.990
And this-- again, this applies both within
a

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:01.649
lab and between labs.

00:20:01.649 --> 00:20:10.040
So even if you were to start adhering to standards
in your lab alone, that gives you benefits,

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.629
because it's easier to collaborate within
this lab with your peers.

00:20:13.629 --> 00:20:17.510
But it's also easier if you're in a higher
position.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:24.200
For example, you're a PI, it's easier to move
one person from a project to another or basically

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:28.580
reuse some
historical data acquired in your lab for a

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:29.860
new project.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:33.700
We also wanted to enable automatic analysis
of data sets.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:37.450
So when you have enough metadata, and they
are all

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:46.720
formally described, that allows you to run
analysis automatically.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:48.910
And you can start with small use cases, when
it

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:54.970
just pre-processed that data in an automatic
way, and you extract the metadata.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.130
And this is much less error
prone.

00:20:57.130 --> 00:21:04.950
But the big-picture vision is that we will
be able to go from data to actual knowledge

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:07.960
through semantic
knowledge in an automatic way, if we have

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:11.210
good standards for describing the data itself.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:17.299
And then finally, we'd like to have tools
for checking consistency of data sets.

00:21:17.299 --> 00:21:22.510
And this is-- this is important in
context of data-sharing, where you're receiving

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:24.460
data from different sides and different groups.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:30.129
And you want to
basically validate them relatively quickly

00:21:30.129 --> 00:21:33.639
and in an automatic way.

00:21:33.639 --> 00:21:39.039
And you also need to think about the players,
so

00:21:39.039 --> 00:21:43.049
you need to have in mind the people that you're
building this for.

00:21:43.049 --> 00:21:44.999
And this will be very important later on when
you

00:21:44.999 --> 00:21:54.490
decide what groups to actually contact, and
who are the key players for this thing to

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:56.379
be successful?

00:21:56.379 --> 00:22:01.330
So we wanted this to be useful for lab PIs,
and they said this is not just reducing errors

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:06.399
and data handling but also,
very importantly, enable reuse of data within

00:22:06.399 --> 00:22:08.470
your own lab.

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:11.419
And might sound a bit weird that I'm stressing
out

00:22:11.419 --> 00:22:20.149
[INAUDIBLE] within a lab use case, but with
this project, we had to find this perceived

00:22:20.149 --> 00:22:26.610
notion that this is only useful
if you are planning to share data publicly

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:27.780
or widely.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:29.470
And this is not the case.

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:35.629
It's a separate orthogonal issue, which
I'm happy to talk to any of you at length

00:22:35.629 --> 00:22:37.009
later.

00:22:37.009 --> 00:22:40.379
But standardization help, whether you are
willing to share data

00:22:40.379 --> 00:22:43.250
publicly or not.

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:45.700
And then we have to talk about pipeline developers.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:48.970
Those are the people who build tools for processing
data, so

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:56.110
they know very well which pieces of methods
are necessary to be able to process the data.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:23:00.100
And then finally
databases and repositories-- so those are

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:05.149
both developers of the software, so curators
and managers of these

00:23:05.149 --> 00:23:10.450
resources-- and because that enables automatic
data submission.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:17.169
And they know very well what pieces of method
they are ask for, and they have been dealing

00:23:17.169 --> 00:23:20.740
with a broad variety of data sets.

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:31.509
OK, and just to drive home that message of
reuse within your own lab, I usually-- when

00:23:31.509 --> 00:23:35.759
talking to a new audience
about the standard-- I present this hypothetical

00:23:35.759 --> 00:23:42.490
situation of Professor Smith, who's up and
coming researcher, that

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:44.669
is running her own lab.

00:23:44.669 --> 00:23:52.409
And she just came up with a new way of reusing
old data that hasn't acquired in her lab a

00:23:52.409 --> 00:23:55.929
while ago.

00:23:55.929 --> 00:24:02.499
And she remembers that this data was acquired
by a particular PhD student, who already graduated.

00:24:02.499 --> 00:24:11.350
And unfortunately the student, Mike, decided
to become a business analyst or a data scientist

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:15.309
in other words in
California.

00:24:15.309 --> 00:24:21.460
And he's completely unreachable, and there's
no way to get him involved in this project.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:26.119
And Professor Smith is trying to make sense
out of her own data, the data that was acquired

00:24:26.119 --> 00:24:30.340
in her own lab, but is
struggling with getting lost with this, because

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:33.509
she found eventually this external hard drive
lying around

00:24:33.509 --> 00:24:35.039
somewhere.

00:24:35.039 --> 00:24:41.679
And it's a heap of incomplete DICOMs and Excel
spreadsheets.

00:24:41.679 --> 00:24:48.649
So this is basically the use case
we're trying to avoid, even within the single

00:24:48.649 --> 00:24:50.679
lab space.

00:24:50.679 --> 00:24:58.809
So moving on we also try to acknowledge what
makes such endeavors successful.

00:24:58.809 --> 00:25:03.679
And many of these things that
we learn on the job-- but hopefully some of

00:25:03.679 --> 00:25:07.020
these will apply to future standardization
projects.

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:14.009
Really wanted to
involve broad scientific community.

00:25:14.009 --> 00:25:19.460
And that basically means that you have to
step outside of the bubble of your

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:21.899
own lab.

00:25:21.899 --> 00:25:23.899
This is the essence of standardization.

00:25:23.899 --> 00:25:26.749
You need to find the common language.

00:25:26.749 --> 00:25:30.960
So if you only speak to
people who are just next to you, that is much

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:36.110
easier, but that doesn't enable this long-range
communication.

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:39.649
And
here the concept of distance is much more

00:25:39.649 --> 00:25:41.190
abstract.

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:43.909
So that means talking to different labs and
talking to different

00:25:43.909 --> 00:25:49.899
researchers, even those that you would normally
not actually interact with, and trying to

00:25:49.899 --> 00:25:51.929
figure out what are their
needs?

00:25:51.929 --> 00:25:53.899
What are their experimental procedures?

00:25:53.899 --> 00:25:57.330
So on and so forth.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:01.279
And then sharing the credit of this work is
very important.

00:26:01.279 --> 00:26:04.549
That's one of the big changes or differences
from

00:26:04.549 --> 00:26:12.139
building standards in an industry and academia
is that-- and the industry usually have those

00:26:12.139 --> 00:26:15.460
panels where
different companies would send their employees

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:21.429
to participate and make things work and find
the consensus.

00:26:21.429 --> 00:26:23.899
And
so basically I have employees that are paid

00:26:23.899 --> 00:26:26.350
to work on the standard.

00:26:26.350 --> 00:26:30.440
In academia, this is a little bit different.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:34.460
And
usually working towards standardization is

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:41.240
not something that is rewarded by the same
academic means.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.950
So
that's why it's very important to share the

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:49.249
credit of the work of a standard.

00:26:49.249 --> 00:26:54.590
So it means that you have to be truly
collaborative and acknowledge all of the individuals

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:58.960
that were involved in the standard in the
way that is respecting

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:04.940
academia, so I'm talking here about authorships
of papers and other products.

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.700
Then focusing on the use cases is extremely
important so--

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:48.190
Hello, everyone.

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:49.321
This is Jack Van Horn.

00:27:49.321 --> 00:27:53.149
We're going to try and get Chris unmuted here
in just a second.

00:27:53.149 --> 00:28:11.049
Hang on
just one moment.

00:28:11.049 --> 00:28:45.450
Hang on just one second folks.

00:28:45.450 --> 00:29:05.710
We're going to try and get Chris back here
in a second.

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:16.029
OK, can you hear me now?

00:29:16.029 --> 00:29:17.190
Yes, we can Chris.

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:18.190
Thanks.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:22.149
Sorry for that [? demo-nitis, ?] I guess.

00:29:22.149 --> 00:29:26.690
So I will assume that I got cut out at 9:33.

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:36.029
Well that's-- well, anyway, the
same slide.

00:29:36.029 --> 00:29:39.899
So back on track.

00:29:39.899 --> 00:29:44.129
So focusing on the use cases, this is what
I was talking about.

00:29:44.129 --> 00:29:51.140
It's basically having in mind the
practical uses of the standard in the future.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:56.110
And that's why it's very important to get
involved all these people that

00:29:56.110 --> 00:30:02.950
will be reusing that data or building tools
to reuse the data involved in this.

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:07.019
Then you have to make the process
public and also document all of your calls

00:30:07.019 --> 00:30:10.549
for comments and different outreach operations.

00:30:10.549 --> 00:30:18.559
And that basically gives
your effort much more validity.

00:30:18.559 --> 00:30:21.840
And then we try to follow this 80/20 rule.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:28.750
And this is basically-- oh, sorry, this basically
means that we are focusing

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:38.070
on 80% of the most common cases, instead of
spending 80% of the time on the tail of 20%

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:41.450
of the cases that are
least common.

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:47.869
And in other words, we basically don't want
the strive for perfection to prevent us from

00:30:47.869 --> 00:30:50.450
delivering
something that is good.

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:55.360
And this is something that you have to remind
yourself over and over again when dealing

00:30:55.360 --> 00:31:02.669
with these things, because quite often it's
easy to be overwhelmed by the amount of details

00:31:02.669 --> 00:31:06.619
and amount of edge
cases that you have to cover.

00:31:06.619 --> 00:31:10.889
And that can slow things down actually to
a halt.

00:31:10.889 --> 00:31:14.519
So that's something that is
important.

00:31:14.519 --> 00:31:19.320
And finally provide tools.

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:25.029
So the standard itself, if you don't provide
any tools, is just a long, boring document

00:31:25.029 --> 00:31:31.010
but
was important to be able to help people adhere

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.489
to this standard, as to provide validators.

00:31:33.489 --> 00:31:36.340
And this is something
that the World Wide Web Consortium excelled

00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:40.590
very-- in a great degree.

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:45.919
They have validators for their markups
down there, and that allows developers writing

00:31:45.919 --> 00:31:50.299
websites to check whether what they have written
adheres to the

00:31:50.299 --> 00:31:51.299
standard.

00:31:51.299 --> 00:31:54.639
And I think that's very important to have
for any standard.

00:31:54.639 --> 00:31:56.769
And then community.

00:31:56.769 --> 00:32:02.529
It's extremely important to basically build
a community around your effort to make all

00:32:02.529 --> 00:32:08.600
devices
feel heard and have a healthy conversation

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:10.070
about different things.

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:12.389
You have to remember, all these people will
be

00:32:12.389 --> 00:32:22.129
donating their free time to this effort, so
it's very important to make them feel appreciated

00:32:22.129 --> 00:32:24.950
as well.

00:32:24.950 --> 00:32:28.559
And building
community is probably the hardest thing about

00:32:28.559 --> 00:32:32.990
coming up with standards in academia, but
it's also the most

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:36.989
important aspect.

00:32:36.989 --> 00:32:40.889
And on the topic of community, you might be
asking how do I get people involved?

00:32:40.889 --> 00:32:43.909
How do I get more people
involved?

00:32:43.909 --> 00:32:50.110
And there are some practical, technical things
that you can do to make that happen.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:55.980
For example, you
should strive to ease the barrier to provide

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:57.040
feedback.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.299
So in other words you're gonna have some draft
documents.

00:32:59.299 --> 00:33:02.850
You're going to have some proposals, some
ideas.

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:05.980
And those ideas need to mature, and you need
to have as

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:14.169
much feedback from the community as possible
to be able to turn this imperfect idea to

00:33:14.169 --> 00:33:18.360
something that is much
more usable and capture perspectives outside

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:19.999
their own bubble.

00:33:19.999 --> 00:33:25.270
So practically here we wanted to make it easy
for people to communicate.

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:33.039
We decided for an old-school mailing
list, because a lot of experts in the field

00:33:33.039 --> 00:33:39.169
are very accustomed with this way of exchanging
ideas.

00:33:39.169 --> 00:33:43.769
But you also went
for an alternative to that we follow in parallel,

00:33:43.769 --> 00:33:48.739
which is an online Google document, that is
open for everyone and

00:33:48.739 --> 00:33:51.739
anyone to comment, even anonymously.

00:33:51.739 --> 00:33:54.970
And that's a great way because you can just
send a link to this

00:33:54.970 --> 00:34:01.989
document, and I can actually show you how
this works right now.

00:34:01.989 --> 00:34:07.230
You can send a link to this document.

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:16.870
And they can basically, immediately, not only
have a look at the currentworking

00:34:16.870 --> 00:34:28.080
version of the standard, but they can also
immediately say-- sorry, they can immediately

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:31.610
say what they
think about it.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:41.611
So in other words, they can go here, and they
can start commenting on the format as it is.

00:34:41.611 --> 00:34:46.820
And as
you can see there are plenty of people that

00:34:46.820 --> 00:34:53.290
already left their comment-- as soon as this
loads-- and this is a quick

00:34:53.290 --> 00:35:03.050
way of getting feedback from the community
that is outside of the mailing list, and also

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:08.510
provides ways to give
direct suggestions to the wording of particular

00:35:08.510 --> 00:35:12.440
definitions, for example.

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:18.190
And then on the topic of credit, it's very
important to list all the contributors by

00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:26.600
name if they are willing to do so and
acknowledge all types of contributions.

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:29.900
So there's going to be plenty of work with
any standard.

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:31.340
And some of it is
documentation.

00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:33.180
Some of it is building examples.

00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:35.140
Some of it is building tools.

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:41.400
And acknowledging all of this hard
volunteer work is very important to make a

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:42.930
thriving community.

00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:45.570
And also induces a feeling of shared ownership.

00:35:45.570 --> 00:35:54.270
And this is something very important, because
people should know that this is a collaborative

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.270
product.

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:58.930
This is
something that only came about because of

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:02.290
a lot of dedication from many people.

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:05.770
And then it's important to organize in-person
meetings.

00:36:05.770 --> 00:36:10.240
Some things are just much better done when
you meet

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:11.540
face-to-face.

00:36:11.540 --> 00:36:17.850
And some discussions can go really badly only
because they're done over email.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:21.130
And finally be
persistent, because most people will be too

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:22.930
busy to help you out to begin with.

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:28.290
And a lot of people will be skeptical
in the beginning, because no one knows if

00:36:28.290 --> 00:36:33.360
your idea in this particular standardization
product you're working on

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:38.230
actually is going to be picked up-- is going
to become a standard.

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:41.401
Until it's widely adopted, it's just a proposal,
so

00:36:41.401 --> 00:36:46.600
people don't know if they should dedicate
time to it in the beginning.

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:49.960
So lots of patience.

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:53.520
So briefly what is BIDS in more detail?

00:36:53.520 --> 00:37:00.330
I'm going to skim through this, and it's a
way of structuring, annotating, and

00:37:00.330 --> 00:37:02.280
organizing data.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:08.970
So in other words, it's not just a set of
definitions of metadata fields, but it's also

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:15.210
how particular
files should be named and organized in a particular

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:17.470
storage space.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:23.450
And this sounds-- might sound a little bit
backwards to some people, especially in the

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:25.230
age of object stars.

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:27.500
But we found that this is something that a
lot of

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:30.810
labs already do.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:36.790
And that particular approach was driven by
the community need.

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:46.350
Standards basically describes binary imaging
data, as well as demographic data for participants

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:47.500
and scans.

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:52.200
And
for that we use TSV files, we use Nifty files,

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:55.910
previously mentioned, for imaging data.

00:37:55.910 --> 00:38:00.850
And then JSON for other
metadata and all of these fields are well

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:03.750
defined in the specification itself.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:10.460
I'm not going to go too much into the
details of BIDS itself, because I would like

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:14.880
the messages here to be much more general,
but I want to talk a little

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:22.810
bit about the tools that are important for
making BIDS happen.

00:38:22.810 --> 00:38:27.910
And obviously there will be APIs, and there
are APIs being built for BIDS that allow you

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:31.210
to query those data set
programmatically in various languages, such

00:38:31.210 --> 00:38:33.410
as Python or Matlab.

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:39.860
And that's important tooling that makes
adoption easier.

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:40.860
That is very important.

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:45.700
But at the same time the standard itself has
to be easy enough for those

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:50.090
tools to be built quickly and easily.

00:38:50.090 --> 00:38:54.910
In other words, you cannot build all of these
APIs for all of the languages

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:55.910
yourself.

00:38:55.910 --> 00:39:03.050
So you should not rely on the fact that everyone
interacting with the data, described using

00:39:03.050 --> 00:39:07.940
a standard,
has to use your tool.

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:12.830
And then converters-- there's a lot of idiosyncrasies
and the data that needs to be converted to

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:14.170
the standard.

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:16.960
And
that's why converters are extremely important.

00:39:16.960 --> 00:39:21.500
And finally validators-- and I cannot stress
it enough, the role of the

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:24.370
BIDS validator is very important.

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:30.930
It basically allows you-- and I will attempt
to demonstrate this right now.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:36.230
It allows
you to, on your own machine, point to the

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:40.890
folder on your hard drive with the data set.

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:44.720
And it performs validation of
the data set client side.

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:45.720
There's no data shared.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:48.410
This is all in the browser using JavaScript
but without any data

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:49.810
transfer.

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:55.760
And it shows you what sort of errors, what
sort of missing metadata pieces there are

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:57.560
in your data set.

00:39:57.560 --> 00:40:04.930
And that takes you from reading a boring specification
to actually adhering to a standard much quicker.

00:40:04.930 --> 00:40:11.480
And then we have a process for creating extensions.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:13.970
That's another important aspect of standardization--
is that

00:40:13.970 --> 00:40:18.170
this is always a live document, so always
will be extensions.

00:40:18.170 --> 00:40:22.150
And it follows a standard procedure that is
similar in

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:26.880
IEEE and World Wide Web Consortium, where
there are trust, there experts involved, there

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.520
are calls for public
comments.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:34.230
And then you create examples, and we extend
the validator.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:38.170
And we strive for a consensus to-- before
we merge it.

00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:42.680
And we are very big on backwards compatibility,
especially because there are so many tools

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:46.330
building
on it right now.

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:51.690
And many of these extensions are being driven
by certain individuals that can pick up the

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:54.970
role of a chairs of the
subcommittees.

00:40:54.970 --> 00:40:56.990
And I just want to list some of them here.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:59.620
Cyril Pernet, Melanie Ganz, and Dora Hermes,
Tal

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:03.530
Yarkoni are working on some of these extensions.

00:41:03.530 --> 00:41:07.660
And there are many others not listed here.

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:12.970
And some of these
extensions actually lead to independent academic

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:14.440
products.

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:21.270
So for example extending BIDS to MEG has been
described as a paper that is currently under

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:22.270
review.

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:29.270
And this is work driven by Galomar Niso from
McGill.

00:41:29.270 --> 00:41:37.400
But to wrap it up, because I feel that time
is running out, I want to talk a little bit

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:41.180
about the use cases enabled by
BIDS, because standardization is all great

00:41:41.180 --> 00:41:44.390
just to have a language to talk to each other.

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:50.600
But what cool new things
can we actually do when we have this standardization.

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:55.150
And one of the things that we started doing
very early on is

00:41:55.150 --> 00:42:02.210
trying to build a standard-- sorry, containerized
applications for doing data processing that

00:42:02.210 --> 00:42:07.240
can take a
standardized BIDS data set.

00:42:07.240 --> 00:42:11.230
So this is a concept kind of similar to what
you have on your phone.

00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:17.840
When you install an app on your phone, you
are never worried about dependencies or other

00:42:17.840 --> 00:42:21.030
software that this app relies on.

00:42:21.030 --> 00:42:27.430
So you marry this concept, which
is called software containers, with taking

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:34.200
standardized inputs as-- sorry, taking standardized
datasets as inputs.

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:41.560
So that allows us to create a set of [INAUDIBLE]
processing applications that require minimal

00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:47.641
metadata inputs and
allow you to very quickly test and also in

00:42:47.641 --> 00:42:52.990
a very reproducible way different analysis
and different pipelines on your

00:42:52.990 --> 00:42:58.140
data set, as long as it is in the standard
way.

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:06.690
And the other thing that we also did marrying
different aspects of these concepts is that

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:11.020
we've created a free
online platform for sharing an analysis of

00:43:11.020 --> 00:43:12.500
neuroimaging data.

00:43:12.500 --> 00:43:19.490
And this platform builds on many of the components
that I already spoke about.

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:25.040
But the important take-home message here is
that the components such as the

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:28.370
validator, such as the BIDS apps, are all
independent.

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:30.480
And they are basic building blocks that other
people can

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:39.430
use to incorporate this into their own systems
and services and website and tools.

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:44.740
And I think that's an important
thing to emulate, because that-- again, this

00:43:44.740 --> 00:43:49.880
is not about a single lab, this is not about
a particular set of individuals.

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:52.250
You're trying to build an ecosystem.

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:56.960
And to do this right you have to make your
components and tools reusable.

00:43:56.960 --> 00:44:04.380
All right, so going back to open neural, this
is a web platform that uses BIDS apps and

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:09.320
uses BIDS to make datasharing,
data analysis, easier.

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:18.380
So basically it allows users to upload data
sets to this website, OpenNeuro.org.

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:20.740
And
then again the validator-- exactly the same

00:44:20.740 --> 00:44:24.680
code base I showed you before-- is being run
before you upload the

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:30.210
data, so you know if there are any errors
that you have to correct before actually committing

00:44:30.210 --> 00:44:34.660
to a long data
transfer process.

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:40.850
And then it allows you to basically-- when
you upload your data set and create a snapshot

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:47.060
of it, it allows you to
choose from a library of different BIDS apps

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:52.590
or basically containerized data processing
pipelines, and you can

00:44:52.590 --> 00:45:03.230
schedule a job running those BIDS apps in
the cloud that is managed by this website.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:05.970
And this is basically how you
can schedule the job and select different

00:45:05.970 --> 00:45:06.970
subjects.

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:11.530
And when you press Submit, you're going to
get an email after

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:21.110
the job is finished, and you can go and explore
results of such analysis.

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:25.880
And I can show you before we move on a
little bit how such analysis actually looks

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:26.880
like.

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:29.070
So here we are on OpenNeuro.

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:34.880
And I uploaded my own brain, because vanity,
I guess.

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:39.990
No, it's because I can
actually, clearly share my own brain dataset

00:45:39.990 --> 00:45:45.060
and other project.

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:55.740
And basically as you can see I can go to different
data sets and look at different parts of the

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:56.740
website.

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:59.750
And we have both public and private data sets.

00:45:59.750 --> 00:46:05.180
But let me just
go to my very own data.

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:15.920
And after a moment, I can show you actually
the jobs as they're finished.

00:46:15.920 --> 00:46:21.150
For some reason, the internet is acting up
today a little bit.

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:24.120
And I'm not quite sure-- OK, there we go.

00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:28.880
So I run a set
of different apps on this particular data

00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:29.880
set.

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:32.980
And for example here, this is ANTs cortical
thickness app-- would

00:46:32.980 --> 00:46:33.980
finish and succeeded.

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:42.640
I can go through the logs, and I can also
look or download through the results.

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:45.710
And for
example, one of the outputs of this particular

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:53.710
pipeline is this image of the brain segmentation.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:57.170
And when it loads,
you're basically going to see how well ANTs,

00:46:57.170 --> 00:47:02.720
which is a particular piece of software for
doing structural and

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:09.740
functional brain coregistration and segmentation,
worked out on this data set.

00:47:09.740 --> 00:47:16.680
So, yes, this is how this works.

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:25.650
And just to wrap up, I would like to thank
many or all of the contributors to BIDS

00:47:25.650 --> 00:47:26.650
and the BIDS ecosystems.

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:33.030
You can see, it's a long and growing names,
long and growing list of names and

00:47:33.030 --> 00:47:37.280
institutions that contributed in different
ways to this effort.

00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:39.870
And we hope that this list is going to grow
even more,

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:44.990
and we're going to make even better standards.

00:47:44.990 --> 00:47:52.720
I also compiled a list of resources that you
can have a look if you want to learn a little

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:55.390
bit more about the things
that I talked about today.

00:47:55.390 --> 00:48:02.920
And that includes the IEEE and W3C processes,
as well as a history of DICOM, and

00:48:02.920 --> 00:48:06.680
some of the BIDS stuff that I talked about.

00:48:06.680 --> 00:48:11.070
And I guess this is all from me.

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:15.000
And I believe that we have some time to
take questions.

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:20.550
Chris, thank you so much for that really great
overview of the need for standards and why

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:25.840
we need to have them
from a practical standpoint and some of the

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:29.280
sociological issues, as well some of these--
providing some of these

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:30.820
resources for us.

00:48:30.820 --> 00:48:38.330
One question that I guess I have is very often,
you'll see where there's a new standard that's

00:48:38.330 --> 00:48:44.140
being announced, and there's some group someplace
that says oh, we've decided that there needs

00:48:44.140 --> 00:48:45.410
to be a
standard.

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:49.840
And we've anointed ourselves as the standard
creators, and we're going to make the standard.

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:50.840
And
you'll all love it.

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:52.120
It'll be great.

00:48:52.120 --> 00:48:53.120
You should want it.

00:48:53.120 --> 00:49:02.000
As an end user, how can one be comfortable
with this-- almost this barrage of standards

00:49:02.000 --> 00:49:04.820
development and
knowing which one is the most trustworthy?

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:07.140
Or is it needed?

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:12.400
And do I make the leap to change the way I
do my

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:17.830
own data standardization, or rather what form
I put my data in?

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:20.060
How do I know when is the time to do this,
and

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:24.090
how do I know to trust the people who are
saying that oh, we've developed this standard,

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:25.090
and it's great?

00:49:25.090 --> 00:49:26.090
You
should adopt it.

00:49:26.090 --> 00:49:27.800
How do I know?

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:31.500
Well, such an easy question to start with.

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:34.390
Yeah, of course I'm kidding.

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:38.220
It's an incredibly hard problem.

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:41.940
So the
telltale sign is adoption, but that's a chicken

00:49:41.940 --> 00:49:47.010
and egg game, because someone has to be the
first one to do it.

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:53.410
So
the other thing that you can go with is seeing

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:58.540
which individuals or which organizations are
actually involved in this

00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:08.120
process, and if there is a process, if there
actually has been a broad communication about

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:10.460
how this has been
developed.

00:50:10.460 --> 00:50:17.910
But at the end of the day, everyone adopting
something new is taking a risk, because it

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:21.230
might be good
or might not be.

00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:24.910
So there are ways to minimize that risk.

00:50:24.910 --> 00:50:31.310
If you are-- it's probably good to commit
with a smaller

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:40.140
project, maybe delegate it for a new RA collecting
a new data set and gather some information

00:50:40.140 --> 00:50:44.910
this way, rather
than committing a whole consortium to it to

00:50:44.910 --> 00:50:45.910
begin with.

00:50:45.910 --> 00:50:47.330
But, yes, this is the struggle.

00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:53.480
And that's why-- especially in the early days
of BIDS-- we struggled to get people

00:50:53.480 --> 00:50:59.220
involved, because they were hesitant, because
they didn't know if this thing's going to

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:00.280
pick up or not.

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:05.940
Well, I guess everybody has a finite energy
that they can devote to things.

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:12.350
And if it looks like they have to do a
whole scale-- a transformation of the way

00:51:12.350 --> 00:51:16.720
they organize their data or storage or what
have you, that I don't know--

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:19.460
for some people that might be a horrifying
thought.

00:51:19.460 --> 00:51:22.640
One thing they might be thinking is that well,
wait a minute.

00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:23.650
Here's some new standard.

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:26.120
Is this based on something I already know?

00:51:26.120 --> 00:51:32.350
So for example one of our listeners is asking
is the data set description based on the HCLS,

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:35.270
W3C data set
description?

00:51:35.270 --> 00:51:39.010
And somebody may say oh, I'm familiar with
that, so OK this makes sense to me.

00:51:39.010 --> 00:51:40.730
And that might
lower their barrier.

00:51:40.730 --> 00:51:45.960
On the other hand, if it's-- I'm not speaking
about BIDS in particular, but if people weren't

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:47.770
familiar with that, they may say well, wait
a minute.

00:51:47.770 --> 00:51:49.530
Why would I go and do this, because it seems
to be

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:54.280
unattached to anything else that I might be
familiar with?

00:51:54.280 --> 00:51:57.670
Is there any sociological barrier there?

00:51:57.670 --> 00:51:59.280
Yeah, totally.

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:04.400
And I think that what is important here is
that they totally agree with that-- those

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:09.700
links that building on
existing solutions is very important.

00:52:09.700 --> 00:52:17.980
But it's also important to acknowledge that
different communities will respect

00:52:17.980 --> 00:52:25.880
and acknowledge or actually be familiar with
different existing solutions.

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:31.980
So for example there are some unwritten
practices in the way that people organize

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:35.640
their data in academic labs, that they are
familiar with.

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:40.440
There is the
imaging data storage format called NIFTI that

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:43.260
a lot of people are familiar with.

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:48.540
And there are some excellent
standardization approaches of data in the

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:52.560
industry that academics might not be familiar
with.

00:52:52.560 --> 00:52:54.110
So it's not only
building on things that are already there

00:52:54.110 --> 00:52:55.110
but also acknowledging what your community
is familiar with, and what

00:52:55.110 --> 00:53:01.210
building on things that are already there
but also acknowledging what your community

00:53:01.210 --> 00:53:06.370
is familiar with, and what
they're gonna basically take advantage of.

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:12.820
And how big of a role do, say, journals play
in this?

00:53:12.820 --> 00:53:18.410
And well there's a lot of interesting sociology
regarding that,

00:53:18.410 --> 00:53:25.370
which we don't need to get into-- about for-profit
or open science or bioarchive or what have

00:53:25.370 --> 00:53:26.370
you.

00:53:26.370 --> 00:53:29.360
But journals play
an important role here in that they're publishing

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:30.530
the research.

00:53:30.530 --> 00:53:33.360
Do they have any sort of-- I don't know what
your

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:39.220
experience has been-- have they had any sort
of buy-in to the acceptance of standards in

00:53:39.220 --> 00:53:42.860
support of the literature
that they publish, so that the data that's

00:53:42.860 --> 00:53:45.910
contained in them is more easily reproducible?

00:53:45.910 --> 00:53:52.570
Yes, so in a way, it was rather indirect.

00:53:52.570 --> 00:53:59.710
And there were two aspects where journals
actually contributed to this.

00:53:59.710 --> 00:54:03.690
One was when we were trying to publish the
standard itself as a paper.

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:11.450
They've pushed that in the right direction
to be more transparent about all the things

00:54:11.450 --> 00:54:14.230
that we did to reach out to the community.

00:54:14.230 --> 00:54:21.450
And I think that was very
important and emphasized what we knew already.

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:25.030
But it's better to make this all transparent.

00:54:25.030 --> 00:54:32.920
And the other aspect how journals are involved
in this is there's this new breed of journals

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:35.100
that publish data.

00:54:35.100 --> 00:54:38.530
So
those are data journals in other words.

00:54:38.530 --> 00:54:42.160
I'm talking about scientific data here, Data
in Brief.

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:43.300
Frontiers is doing that.

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:44.930
Neuroinformatics is doing that.

00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:54.520
There are specialized data journals for social
sciences and psychology as well.

00:54:54.520 --> 00:55:02.140
And those journals, since they publish data,
or data descriptors, they care a lot about

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:07.100
the consistency and the
reusability of those data sets.

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:24.390
And they usually offload the burden of data--
over indirect.

00:55:24.390 --> 00:55:32.090
As for example, there are
a few journals that would recommend repositories

00:55:32.090 --> 00:55:34.220
that adhere to standards.

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:36.270
But they wouldn't recommend
standards themselves.

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:44.040
So this is how journals basically play a role
in this.

00:55:44.040 --> 00:55:48.030
Right, well, we're at the top of the hour
now.

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:53.810
And I'd like to thank you, Chris, for providing
this great overview of

00:55:53.810 --> 00:55:56.680
data description standards for biomedicine.

00:55:56.680 --> 00:56:05.040
A perfect addition to our collection of webinars
in the Guide to the

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:07.260
Fundamentals of Data Science.

00:56:07.260 --> 00:56:09.560
I'd like to thank everybody for joining in.

00:56:09.560 --> 00:56:16.040
Once again, thanking Chris and do look
forward to a future of our Guide to the Fundamentals

00:56:16.040 --> 00:56:18.540
of Data Science Webinars.

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:22.000
And watch your email for further
announcements about those.

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:23.000
Wonderful.

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:24.000
Thank you very much.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:25.400
Everybody have a wonderful day and a great
weekend.

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:26.400
Thank you.

